GStreamer Daemon, also called gstd, is a GStreamer framework for
controlling audio and video streaming using TCP connection
messages. Qt applications, web interfaces, and GStreamer element test
suites show different ways the framework can be utilized. GStreamer
Daemon is gst-launch on steroids where you can create a GStreamer
pipeline, play, pause, change speed, skip around, and even change
element parameter settings all while the pipeline is active.

GStreamer Daemon is designed for production deployment, where the
control logic runs in a separate process for the audio video streaming
server. The control application can be written in any language, does
not need to use gobject / glib, as long as data can be exchanged over
a TCP connection. The control application can run on the same
processor (using localhost for the TCP destination) or a separate
machine. Isolating the control logic from the streaming logic solves
many difficult problems, from setting priorities to avoid audio drop
out to having a working user interface if the streaming application
encounters corrupt data. By separating the audio video streaming
logic, it is easy to create automated A/V tests, extend a product's
features to support remote control, and allow the control application
the streaming daemon to be developed independently.

The official documentation is held in [RidgeRun Developer's Wiki.](http://developer.ridgerun.com/wiki/index.php?title=Gstd-1.0)

## Security hardening (this fork)

The gstd protocol has no built-in authentication: any client that can
reach a socket has full pipeline control, which is equivalent to code
execution as the gstd user (pipelines can read and write files). The
servers bind to 127.0.0.1 by default; this fork adds the following
opt-in hardening on top of that:

- **HTTP API token** — `--http-api-token <token>` or the
  `GSTD_HTTP_API_TOKEN` environment variable (preferred, since command
  lines are visible to other local processes). When set, every HTTP
  request except `GET`/`HEAD /health` must carry an
  `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header. An explicitly empty token is
  a startup error rather than a silent "no authentication".
- **CORS disabled by default** — the server no longer sends
  `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, so web pages in a local browser
  cannot read API responses cross-origin. To allow a trusted web UI,
  set `--http-cors-origin <origin>` or `GSTD_HTTP_CORS_ORIGIN` to exactly
  one origin such as `https://ui.example.com` or `http://127.0.0.1:8080`.
  A wildcard (`*`), a path or trailing slash, a query, credentials, or a
  list is a startup error.
- **Pipeline cap** — `--max-pipelines <count>` or `GSTD_MAX_PIPELINES`
  limits how many pipelines may exist at once (default: unlimited),
  bounding memory/FD exhaustion from runaway clients. Pipelines still
  being built count against the cap, so a burst of parallel creates is
  rejected before the surplus pipelines are constructed.
- **HTTP resource names** (`?name=`) may not contain whitespace or
  control characters, so they can no longer smuggle extra arguments
  into the internal command parser.
- The runtime and log directories (`/var/run/gstd`, `/var/log/gstd`)
  install as mode 1777 (sticky, like `/tmp`) instead of 777, so local
  users cannot delete or replace each other's pid/socket/log files.
  Deployments with a dedicated gstd user should tighten this to 0750.

## CPU clock floor (this fork)

Some CPU frequency governors judge load per core and keep clocks low
while a pipeline whose work is spread over several threads, or
offloaded to hardware, falls behind real time. Setting
`GSTD_PIPELINE_UTIL_CLAMP_MIN` to a value from 1 to 1024 gives gstd's
threads that minimum utilization clamp while at least one pipeline
exists. The clamp is raised when the first pipeline is created and
released when the last one is deleted; threads started in between,
such as streaming threads, inherit it. Threads already clamped higher,
including real-time threads under the kernel's default boost, are left
alone, and releasing only touches threads still at gstd's value.

What the clamp does depends on the governor. `schedutil` and governors
built on the same utilization signal raise the clock whenever a
clamped thread runs: with `schedutil`, `512` asks for roughly 60% of
the maximum clock and `1024` for all of it. Governors such as
`ondemand`, `conservative` and `performance` ignore it. On a CPU with
big and little cores, clamped threads may also be placed on the bigger
cores. A sleeping thread is not affected, so while pipelines are idle
the CPU still clocks down. A cgroup's `cpu.uclamp` limits can cap the
effect, and before Linux 5.14 a container's `cpu.uclamp.min` (0 by
default) could cancel it.

It needs Linux 5.3 or later with utilization clamping
(`CONFIG_UCLAMP_TASK`), and changing a clamp usually requires
`CAP_SYS_NICE`. Without either, gstd logs one warning and runs
unclamped. Unset by default.

## Shutdown (this fork)

`SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` are received by a dedicated thread rather than a
main-loop signal source, so a daemon whose main loop is blocked behind a
pipeline that never finishes a state change still shuts down. The same
thread bounds the shutdown: `--shutdown-timeout <seconds>` (default 5)
is how long gstd waits for pipelines and request handlers to wind down
before it exits anyway, with exit status 1 and a log line. `0` waits
forever, which is the previous behaviour; a second signal exits at once
in either case. `gstd -k` waits for the daemon's deadline plus 2 s, so
pass the same `--shutdown-timeout` to `-k` that the daemon was started
with when it is not the default.
